WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:03.170
Carlton:
You think that you know

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.672
Carlton:
about what happened during
Hurricane Katrina?

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:09.009
♪♪

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.969
You actually really don't.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:13.972
♪♪

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:18.268
Vera:
Five Days At Memorial is a show
about doctors and nurses

00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.187
Vera:
in a hospital, in New Orleans,

00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:21.647
Vera:
during Hurricane Katrina,

00:00:21.647 --> 00:00:27.152
who find themselves
forced to make life and death
choices under extreme duress.

00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:28.904
♪♪

00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:30.822
Sheri:
For me as the journalist,

00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:34.868
the important thing isn't to
judge, it's to find out what
really happened.

00:00:35.619 --> 00:00:39.206
Sheri:
Not making one person a hero
and one person a victim.

00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:41.208
Sheri:
Nurses, doctors, patients,

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:43.877
Sheri:
they were stuck in a horrific
circumstance that was

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.003
Sheri:
not their fault.

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:50.133
Cornelius:
And what made Memorial stand 
out is the bodycount.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.137
Carlton:
After the water was pumped out
of the city,

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:56.765
people went into this hospital
and found 45 dead bodies.

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.309
How did that happen and who was
responsible for that?

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:04.106
A hospital that size,
maybe 45 patients just
succumbed.

00:01:04.106 --> 00:01:05.399
Maybe they didn't.

00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:10.570
Vera:
They keep assuming that someone
is going to come and help them.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.742
Someone from the city, someone
from the state, someone from the
federal government, FEMA.

00:01:16.702 --> 00:01:22.124
We have to consider patients
that we can't evacuate.

00:01:22.124 --> 00:01:26.003
Dr. Mulderick:
We're running out of food and we
will be out of fresh water by tomorrow.

00:01:26.003 --> 00:01:31.967
Life is gray, and when you're in
the position of making life and
death decisions, it's even grayer.

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:34.136
Power's out and the water is
rising.

00:01:34.136 --> 00:01:36.847
Without power, it's not a
hospital, it's just a building.

00:01:36.847 --> 00:01:40.309
Dr. Mulderick:
We need to discuss who's going
first and who's going last.

00:01:40.309 --> 00:01:44.021
Cornelius:
We all have different
perspectives about about what's right and what's wrong.

00:01:44.021 --> 00:01:47.858
I can't imagine having to make
those decisions and be
ultimately responsible.

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:52.571
It's a horrible circumstance
when doctors are sort of forced
to play god.

00:01:52.571 --> 00:01:54.156
♪♪

00:01:54.156 --> 00:01:57.743
Sheri:
I believe that the viewer can
have an opinion.

00:01:58.118 --> 00:01:59.244
There's no easy answers.

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:00.829
♪♪

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.248
Carlton:
You have to give the audience
the opportunity

00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:06.168
Carlton:
to not just be an observer but
also a participant.

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:07.919
I can evac one person.

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:09.296
-One?
-One, hurry!

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.674
What would you do? What
decisions would you make?

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:15.052
These people were heroes.

00:02:15.469 --> 00:02:18.263
They did save hundreds of
patients' lives.

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:21.183
♪♪

00:02:21.183 --> 00:02:25.228
Vera:
But they also had to make
horrible decisions.

